4 608460.4 234346.7  0.01275 0.1122078       0        0 0.01475
5 608851.3 234346.7  0.01725 0.1302178       0        0 0.01525
6 609242.2 234346.7  0.01300 0.1132882       0        0 0.01750
> range(preds$p)
[1] 0.00875 0.03775

Plotting the predictions, this is exactly what it should look like - corroborated by models in INLA, MapGAM, and mgcv:

ggplot(preds) +
  geom_raster(aes(long, lat, fill = Estimate))+
  scale_fill_viridis(option="B") +
  theme_bw()

The yellow area in the center typically has a smooth contour around it for significance compared with the aspatial average. In frequentist GAMs there are other outlying areas of significantly low odds areas too, but the Bayesian methods seem less prone to doing this.

> ggplot(preds) +
+   geom_raster(aes(long, lat, fill = Estimate))+
+   scale_fill_viridis(option = "B") +
+   theme_bw()+
+   geom_contour(aes(x=long, y=lat, z=p), breaks=c(0.025, 0.975), color="Black", cex=1.2, lty=1)

