Subject: Nonconverging model with lots of random effects
Message-ID: <F0SHySWRwHY.AdU2016SCAAJ@brms-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/brms-users/c/F0SHySWRwHY/m/AdU2016SCAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I've got a model with a large number of random effects that isn't converging. Briefly, I have a spatial model with the form:

mod <- brm( result ~ s( long, lat ) + predictor1 + predictor2 + ( 1 | id ), chains=6, iter=3000,
                  control= list(adapt_delta=0.95), family=bernoulli, data=data )

The id term is for each observation (n=19,300) in the dataset, i.e. there is a RE term for each individual. The reason for this is that I suspect the relationship between predictor1 and predictor2 is in itself spatially variable due to unmeasured neighborhood factors, and in adding the individual random effect term I am (hopefully) allowing a random intercept for each of 19,300 unique coordinate pairs. (I'd curbsided a colleague who is a card-carrying Bayesian spatial epidemiologist who suggested this).

The model seems to work ok in INLA, but I've converted this whole study to brms, and I just can't get the model to converge. Using this adapt_delta and adding 6 chains / 3000 iterations per chain the model took around 48 hours to run and I still got this error. I guess the question is should I just not even bother with using the individual random effects terms? I've got a nicely converged result without it ...

Samples: 6 chains, each with iter = 3000; warmup = 1500; thin = 1;
         total post-warmup samples = 9000
    ICs: LOO = Not computed; WAIC = Not computed

Smooth Terms:
                Estimate Est.Error l-95% CI u-95% CI Eff.Sample Rhat
sds(slonglat_1)    18.63     16.37     1.06    65.78        110 1.03

Group-Level Effects:
~id (Number of levels: 19348)
              Estimate Est.Error l-95% CI u-95% CI Eff.Sample Rhat
sd(Intercept)   240.38    206.94    32.96   748.98          5 2.55

Population-Level Effects:
           Estimate Est.Error l-95% CI u-95% CI Eff.Sample Rhat
Intercept   -443.10    384.68 -1385.35   -59.72          5 2.56
predictor1    -2.18      1.89    -7.45    -0.24          5 1.87
predictor2     1.46      1.34     0.13     4.63          5 2.62
slonglat_1     3.02      6.83    -8.80    18.29         13 1.19
slonglat_2    -6.25      9.09   -31.90     4.65          9 1.61

Samples were drawn using sampling(NUTS). For each parameter, Eff.Sample
is a crude measure of effective sample size, and Rhat is the potential
scale reduction factor on split chains (at convergence, Rhat = 1).
Warning messages:
1: The model has not converged (some Rhats are > 1.1). Do not analyse the results!
We recommend running more iterations and/or setting stronger priors.
2: There were 1779 divergent transitions after warmup. Increasing adapt_delta above 0.95 may help.
See http://mc-stan.org/misc/warnings.html#divergent-transitions-after-warmup

