One follow-up on this to be clear. In such a case I wouldnt actually trust the brms results too much. The model is likely overly complex for your data.

Am 31.08.2017 9:19 nachm. schrieb "Daniel Buonaiuto" <dbuon...@g.harvard.edu>:

> There is indeed only one observation/species in the dataset. Thanks for your speedy reply and helpful answer!
>
> On Thursday, August 31, 2017 at 3:00:13 PM UTC-4, Paul Buerkner wrote:
> > How many observations per species does your data contain? If only one, the brms model is only weakly identified, since it sort of models a residual SD via  (1|name) in this case. This whould explain the differences.
> >
> > Apart from that, phyloglm possibly implements the phylgeny in a different way than brms or MCMCglmm doe, but I don't know this package really. I will look into it though.
